# Keep agents' and humans' searches cheap.
#
# `.gitignore` answers "should git track this?". This file answers a different
# question: "should a TEXT SEARCH look at this?". ripgrep honours `.ignore` in
# addition to `.gitignore`, so these exclusions apply to `rg` regardless of what
# git thinks, and to any tool that respects the same convention.
#
# WHY IT MATTERS HERE SPECIFICALLY
#
# `vendor/build-inputs/` holds 524 MB of committed binaries — eight engine and
# service executables plus four geo databases. They are tracked ON PURPOSE (see
# docs/operate/VENDORED-BUILD-INPUTS.md: five outages came from build inputs
# living behind third-party URLs), so `.gitignore` cannot exclude them. But no
# search ever wants their contents, and a grep across the repository wades
# through them every time.
#
# Nothing here is deleted or untracked. This only stops a search from paying for
# files whose contents are not text in the first place.

# ---- Binary build inputs: tracked deliberately, never searched ----
vendor/build-inputs/

# ---- Release output staged for upload; reproducible from the tag ----
release-artifacts/

# ---- Local build state ----
client/target/
client/node_modules/
client/dist/
client/src-tauri/sidecar/
client/src-tauri/gen/
server/console/node_modules/
server/console/dist/
server/site/node_modules/

# ---- Asset binaries: images, fonts, data files ----
*.png
*.jpg
*.jpeg
*.gif
*.ico
*.icns
*.ppm
*.ttf
*.otf
*.woff
*.woff2
*.mmdb
*.dat
*.exe
*.dylib
*.so
*.rlib
*.d

# ---- Generated code and lockfiles: large, and never the answer to "where is
#      this logic?" ----
client/pnpm-lock.yaml
client/Cargo.lock
server/console/pnpm-lock.yaml
server/site/package-lock.json

# ---- Local scratch that is not the repository's business ----
scratch/
.diag/
.hubtmp/
.chrysalis-keys/
.locus-keys/
__pycache__/
